    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "Let <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/7/de7b0c6ea3e31c387633fa07b2b2c195c01d58e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/2/e52558ad68514f196dece9838eabd14034b7be3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" >,</span> ..., <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/f/14fb7898d637e7386091f58bf9d57bc24e86b4bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/e/d4e588aaf392305bab4e130b549e683d9f35ba7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ b_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"15\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/e/64e2c744b98cd3ee8f2514eb745d693e0b95b687.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ b_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" >,</span> ..., <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2be78bf8c20d75c771f4635d5be9cf901d4b8b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ b_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" > be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/9/f39ca614e6beed9720b862df639ee9a298f10980.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ 2 \\cdot n$\"  width=\"32\" height=\"12\" > real numbers. Prove that the following two statements are equivalent:<br>\n<br>\n<b>i)</b> For any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/3/6d3f8b726378d5420223c5cb14b10f24b202b187.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > real numbers <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/5/e7520cb4f852853317f2bc80d75ffcff22156d69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/e/3ce27296c910fac7f5587aa65992cf6ce707e6fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" >,</span> ..., <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/2/5322386e555189c4f90a59a141173a6f2592a81f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" > satisfying <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/6/60601be2938081aeeefe87ef015b22064444757b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x_1 \\leq x_2 \\leq \\ldots \\leq x_ n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"148\" height=\"14\" >,</span> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57e1ca3d0f5eab59469e3823cf8fd44bc2afb9a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\sum^{n}_{k = 1} a_k \\cdot x_k \\leq \\sum^{n}_{k = 1} b_k \\cdot x_k,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"183\" height=\"48\" ><br>\n<br>\n<b>ii)</b> We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/2/39214f1250ca471f47040f514b9a953ca1090836.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\sum^{s}_{k = 1} a_k \\leq \\sum^{s}_{k = 1} b_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"114\" height=\"48\" > for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/9/c59bcfb83e3fbdc6ac56cbb6cbfebe950b0d80dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ s\\in\\left\\{1,2,...,n-1\\right\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"147\" height=\"18\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/7/567ecddf518ff9fcfa8e88781d18f8c2e6b3c80b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\sum^{n}_{k = 1} a_k = \\sum^{n}_{k = 1} b_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"114\" height=\"48\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Let $ a_1$, $ a_2$, ..., $ a_n$ and $ b_1$, $ b_2$, ..., $ b_n$ be $ 2 \\cdot n$ real numbers. Prove that the following two statements are equivalent:\r\n\r\n[b]i)[/b] For any $ n$ real numbers $ x_1$, $ x_2$, ..., $ x_n$ satisfying $ x_1 \\leq x_2 \\leq \\ldots \\leq x_ n$, we have $ \\sum^{n}_{k \\equal{} 1} a_k \\cdot x_k \\leq \\sum^{n}_{k \\equal{} 1} b_k \\cdot x_k,$\r\n\r\n[b]ii)[/b] We have $ \\sum^{s}_{k \\equal{} 1} a_k \\leq \\sum^{s}_{k \\equal{} 1} b_k$ for every $ s\\in\\left\\{1,2,...,n\\minus{}1\\right\\}$ and $ \\sum^{n}_{k \\equal{} 1} a_k \\equal{} \\sum^{n}_{k \\equal{} 1} b_k$.",
    "username": "orl",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_1991.jpg"
   }
