   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 234053,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 37412,
    "poster_id": 5730,
    "post_rendered": "The intention must be that (i) is true for all possible increasing n-tuples <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/8/2e894ebb8c4e2dcb0ad8c2b8d415aaf4a0303e86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" >,</span> otherwise the problem is false.  Also, the inequality in (ii) must go the opposite way, as follows from the proof below.  Here is the corrected version:<br>\n<br>\n<b>[Moderator edit: I have corrected Orl's original post above according to this. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8cc809405164c38b91adffd25eb9c2ab191d2144.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ -$\" style=\"vertical-align: 4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"1\" > Darij]</b><br>\n<br>\nThis problem is very interesting, because (ii) is the condition for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/d/b9d389de6d8a8314b29faf761bb09a117e5f53c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ b$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > to majorize <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/255f65757f75ce300036173cb8e6f8f86dcfe90f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"8\" >,</span> but <i>without</i> the usual condition that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/255f65757f75ce300036173cb8e6f8f86dcfe90f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"8\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/d/b9d389de6d8a8314b29faf761bb09a117e5f53c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ b$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > are ordered.   So it looks like a majorization condition for the class of increasing functions in place of convex ones.  Therefore, there should be several more equivalent conditions such as<br>\n<br>\n(iii) Sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/d/b9d389de6d8a8314b29faf761bb09a117e5f53c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ b$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > can be obtained from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/255f65757f75ce300036173cb8e6f8f86dcfe90f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"8\" > by a series of transformations of the following type: given indices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/b/25b59923b3d1e1a7d912579f476c3a68961264ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ i &lt; j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"38\" height=\"16\" > and a positive real number <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/c/30c00ada12b57ed62be460cddad168c732a453a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ p \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" >,</span> change <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/8/fb82dd1bbf4913cb3e5efdbe46ad92d301a52c5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_i \\to a_i - p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"90\" height=\"13\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/a/51a99f4e0e7e7740e79ad12a3fe39bca935c19d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_j \\to a_j + p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"15\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<b>Question </b>:  what is the analogue of the convex majorization criterion using double stochastic matrices (row and column sums = 1, Birkhoff polytope)?<br>\n<br>\n<br>\nAnyway, the proof for the problem:<br>\n<br>\nInequality (i) holds for all n-tuples <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/8/2e894ebb8c4e2dcb0ad8c2b8d415aaf4a0303e86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > if and only if it holds for constant n-tuples <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/3/ee335ec0837e4593bebb04a947e916c72ab65075.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ (c,c,c,...,c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"18\" > and all n-tuples of the form <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/2/1a258bd96c4d6e1c35f6700389f22ea783312823.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ (0,0,...,1,1,1,...)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" >.</span> This is because any increasing sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/8/2e894ebb8c4e2dcb0ad8c2b8d415aaf4a0303e86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > can be written as a sum (with non-negative coefficients) of such vectors.<br>\n(i) for constant vectors is the condition <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/b/d1b29579ff0d79d22906398454e5a7609657c5c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\Sigma a_i = \\Sigma b_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"15\" >.</span><br>\n(i) for the vector with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/7/da78896b74c30e2c8e56380ae6efb2e4d0855f92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x_i = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" > for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/a/d8a1b83f4cf4ecef3047fbadf64a9efc0d001108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ i &lt; k$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/7/8b7c23481fc180ae6c425d4eefbde87a72152c25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ x_i = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" > for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/0/5b00011a9f2252cdd1b7eba21269186a0808d9e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ i \\geq k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"40\" height=\"15\" >  is the condition <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/9/5e9ab3aa596c3a1586b96fb1ec829e45e2f9d9b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ \\sum^{n}_{i = k} a_i \\leq \\sum^{n}_{i = k} b_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"109\" height=\"48\" >.</span><br>\nThat's all.",
    "post_canonical": "The intention must be that (i) is true for all possible increasing n-tuples $ x$, otherwise the problem is false.  Also, the inequality in (ii) must go the opposite way, as follows from the proof below.  Here is the corrected version:\r\n\r\n[b][Moderator edit: I have corrected Orl's original post above according to this. $ \\minus{}$ Darij][/b]\r\n\r\nThis problem is very interesting, because (ii) is the condition for $ b$ to majorize $ a$, but [i]without[/i] the usual condition that $ a$, $ b$ are ordered.   So it looks like a majorization condition for the class of increasing functions in place of convex ones.  Therefore, there should be several more equivalent conditions such as\r\n\r\n(iii) Sequence $ b$ can be obtained from $ a$ by a series of transformations of the following type: given indices $ i < j$ and a positive real number $ p \\geq 0$, change $ a_i \\to a_i \\minus{} p$ and $ a_j \\to a_j \\plus{} p$.\r\n\r\n[b]Question [/b]:  what is the analogue of the convex majorization criterion using double stochastic matrices (row and column sums = 1, Birkhoff polytope)?\r\n\r\n\r\nAnyway, the proof for the problem:\r\n\r\nInequality (i) holds for all n-tuples $ x$ if and only if it holds for constant n-tuples $ (c,c,c,...,c)$ and all n-tuples of the form $ (0,0,...,1,1,1,...)$. This is because any increasing sequence $ x$ can be written as a sum (with non-negative coefficients) of such vectors.\r\n(i) for constant vectors is the condition $ \\Sigma a_i \\equal{} \\Sigma b_i$.\r\n(i) for the vector with $ x_i \\equal{} 0$ for $ i < k$ and $ x_i \\equal{} 1$ for $ i \\geq k$  is the condition $ \\sum^{n}_{i \\equal{} k} a_i \\leq \\sum^{n}_{i \\equal{} k} b_i$.\r\nThat's all.",
