    "post_rendered": "Given a positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"13\" > written in decimal expansion: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/1/cf18a1198dfbf61a4259372ae977356220aae4b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{n},a_{n-1}, \\ldots, a_{0})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"132\" height=\"18\" > and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/a/03aaf0378607b4e66f9ab51e9af538997ab78cf0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(A)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" > denote <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/2/b725435308b952a18e3daff5f6587402eb19ffe5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum^{n}_{k=0} 2^{n-k}\\cdot a_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"94\" height=\"48\" >.</span> Define <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/f/e1f99363d955d613c86f7715f27cc169ce8b159f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1=f(A), A_2=f(A_1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"182\" height=\"18\" >.</span> Prove that:<br>\n<br>\n<b>I.</b> There exists positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > for which <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/9/7890221226aa5c92e2d5e94b71386c459eea3186.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{k+1}=A_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"17\" >.</span><br>\n<b>II.</b> Find such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/5/7553256f94a2ce1069471dfe7c43bf8b7215ab90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" > for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/6/8667c5b637ee320407c5cbfff43cbb1660590bef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$19^{86}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"15\" >",
    "post_canonical": "Given a positive integer $A$ written in decimal expansion: $(a_{n},a_{n-1}, \\ldots, a_{0})$ and let $f(A)$ denote $\\sum^{n}_{k=0} 2^{n-k}\\cdot a_k$. Define $A_1=f(A), A_2=f(A_1)$. Prove that:\n\n[b]I.[/b] There exists positive integer $k$ for which $A_{k+1}=A_k$.\n[b]II.[/b] Find such $A_k$ for $19^{86}.$",
