    "post_rendered": "Given a triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/4/b94961e45efae021bd6dec9ee5d5e49ca26ba0cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C=90$\"  width=\"56\" height=\"12\" > degrees, prove that given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > points inside it, we can name them <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/b/a6b12811fea4ea8f84da836b265a6ab06e48792a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1, P_2 , \\ldots , P_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" > in some way such that:<br>\n<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/0/3202c86bc635a613f01fa4408619505ebbe39b21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum^{n-1}_{k=1} \\left( P_K P_{k+1} \\right)^2 \\leq AB^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"170\" height=\"50\" > (the sum is over the consecutive square of the segments from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > up to <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" >)</span>.<br>\n<br>\n<i>Edited by orl.</i>",
    "post_canonical": "Given a triangle $ABC$ for which $C=90$ degrees, prove that given $n$ points inside it, we can name them $P_1, P_2 , \\ldots , P_n$ in some way such that:\r\n\r\n$\\sum^{n-1}_{k=1} \\left( P_K P_{k+1} \\right)^2 \\leq AB^2$ (the sum is over the consecutive square of the segments from $1$ up to $n-1$).\r\n\r\n[i]Edited by orl.[/i]",
