  {
   "item_id": 234085,
   "item_text": "Click for solution",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post_hidden",
   "in_feed": null,
   "is_watched": null,
   "item_score": "990",
   "id": "27401",
   "item_level": "990",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 234085,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 37419,
    "poster_id": 432,
    "post_rendered": "Let the excircle of triangle APQ opposite to the vertex A have center C' and touch the sideline AP of the triangle at the point B'. Then, <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/2/6d2b88608e0528e117bec51105da4af052f20eeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C^{\\prime}B^{\\prime}\\perp AP$\"  width=\"90\" height=\"14\" >,</span> and the distance AB' equals the semiperimeter of triangle APQ. Since the perimeter of triangle APQ equals 2, its semiperimeter must be 1; thus, we have AB' = 1. On the other hand, we know that AB = 1 (in fact, the square ABCD has sidelength 1). Since the points B and B' both lie on the ray AP, we thus can conclude that the points B and B' coincide. Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/2/6d2b88608e0528e117bec51105da4af052f20eeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C^{\\prime}B^{\\prime}\\perp AP$\"  width=\"90\" height=\"14\" > becomes <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/3/05399ac43bb0cc4916fa95bd1461634b126a21ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C^{\\prime}B\\perp AP$\"  width=\"85\" height=\"14\" >;</span> in other words, <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/8/0788801d1090073ecb8540d2699295b949cf2354.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C^{\\prime}B\\perp AB$\"  width=\"85\" height=\"14\" >.</span> Hence, the point C' lies on the perpendicular to the line AB at the point B. But since ABCD is a square, the perpendicular to the line AB at the point B is the line BC. Hence, the point C' lies on the line BC. Similarly, the point C' lies on the line DC. As the lines BC and DC have only one common point, namely the point C, it follows that the point C' coincides with the point C. Since we have defined the point C' as the center of the excircle of triangle APQ opposite to the vertex A, it follows that the point C is the center of the excircle of triangle APQ opposite to the vertex A. Thus, this point C lies on the external angle bisector of the angle APQ. Hence, <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/d/e8d1e579bd41becd0601b65e8b9f40d84e3b930e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle CPQ=90^{\\circ}-\\frac{\\measuredangle APQ}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"185\" height=\"38\" >.</span> Similarly, <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/6/6f60a2b6e3638cb15c3e2a73c55b78316c822078.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle CQP=90^{\\circ}-\\frac{\\measuredangle AQP}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"185\" height=\"38\" >.</span> Hence, by the sum of the angles in triangle PCQ, we have<br>\n<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/e/62ec371eb6a2daca0450c221e00926bad03cf647.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle PCQ=180^{\\circ}-\\measuredangle CPQ-\\measuredangle CQP=180^{\\circ}-\\left(90^{\\circ}-\\frac{\\measuredangle APQ}{2}\\right)-\\left(90^{\\circ}-\\frac{\\measuredangle AQP}{2}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"637\" height=\"43\" ><br>\n<span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/3/933ff39fb5dca05b5172123390259c0f599ef416.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$=\\frac{\\measuredangle APQ+\\measuredangle AQP}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"153\" height=\"38\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\nBut since the triangle PAQ is right-angled at A (this is because the quadrilateral ABCD is a square), we have &lt; APQ + &lt; AQP = 90°, and thus <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcf8a6915d1854f098d9ddca497287f482e42114.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle PCQ=\\frac{90^{\\circ}}{2}=45^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"158\" height=\"37\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\ndarij",
    "post_canonical": "Let the excircle of triangle APQ opposite to the vertex A have center C' and touch the sideline AP of the triangle at the point B'. Then, $C^{\\prime}B^{\\prime}\\perp AP$, and the distance AB' equals the semiperimeter of triangle APQ. Since the perimeter of triangle APQ equals 2, its semiperimeter must be 1; thus, we have AB' = 1. On the other hand, we know that AB = 1 (in fact, the square ABCD has sidelength 1). Since the points B and B' both lie on the ray AP, we thus can conclude that the points B and B' coincide. Hence, $C^{\\prime}B^{\\prime}\\perp AP$ becomes $C^{\\prime}B\\perp AP$; in other words, $C^{\\prime}B\\perp AB$. Hence, the point C' lies on the perpendicular to the line AB at the point B. But since ABCD is a square, the perpendicular to the line AB at the point B is the line BC. Hence, the point C' lies on the line BC. Similarly, the point C' lies on the line DC. As the lines BC and DC have only one common point, namely the point C, it follows that the point C' coincides with the point C. Since we have defined the point C' as the center of the excircle of triangle APQ opposite to the vertex A, it follows that the point C is the center of the excircle of triangle APQ opposite to the vertex A. Thus, this point C lies on the external angle bisector of the angle APQ. Hence, $\\measuredangle CPQ=90^{\\circ}-\\frac{\\measuredangle APQ}{2}$. Similarly, $\\measuredangle CQP=90^{\\circ}-\\frac{\\measuredangle AQP}{2}$. Hence, by the sum of the angles in triangle PCQ, we have\r\n\r\n$\\measuredangle PCQ=180^{\\circ}-\\measuredangle CPQ-\\measuredangle CQP=180^{\\circ}-\\left(90^{\\circ}-\\frac{\\measuredangle APQ}{2}\\right)-\\left(90^{\\circ}-\\frac{\\measuredangle AQP}{2}\\right)$\r\n$=\\frac{\\measuredangle APQ+\\measuredangle AQP}{2}$.\r\n\r\nBut since the triangle PAQ is right-angled at A (this is because the quadrilateral ABCD is a square), we have < APQ + < AQP = 90°, and thus $\\measuredangle PCQ=\\frac{90^{\\circ}}{2}=45^{\\circ}$.\r\n\r\n  darij",
    "username": "darij grinberg",
