    "poster_id": 26,
    "post_rendered": "Fix <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/0/250f765e0f0b3842f9d095dc0a3569905d344ce8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E,F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"36\" height=\"16\" >,</span> and move <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > on <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"13\" >.</span> If we regard the area/perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/a/6ca8e1ed0a0d34754cdd985dfc1d6683a066da7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EFG$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"12\" > as a function of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/2/f02853b982d315768fdc5b4b85f92f570155a8aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=AG$\"  width=\"62\" height=\"13\" >,</span> then it's easy to see that this function is convex, so it reaches its maximum in one of the endpoints of the segment <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"13\" >.</span> We can thus move <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > to a vertex of the tetrahedron and increase the area/perimeter. We repeat the procedure until all three vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/c/15cb11a443e820bf5072a194e2dae70dc5234d51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E,F,G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" > are placed in some vertices of the tetrahedron, and the conclusion follows.",
    "post_canonical": "Fix $E,F$, and move $G$ on $AD$. If we regard the area/perimeter of $EFG$ as a function of $x=AG$, then it's easy to see that this function is convex, so it reaches its maximum in one of the endpoints of the segment $AD$. We can thus move $G$ to a vertex of the tetrahedron and increase the area/perimeter. We repeat the procedure until all three vertices $E,F,G$ are placed in some vertices of the tetrahedron, and the conclusion follows.",
